WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.669
-Our first guest is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.504
actress and director
whose hit podcast

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:08.274
"Say More with Dr? Sheila"
is available now.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:09.575
You can also see her

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:12.578
along with Tina Fey
on their "Restless Leg tour,"

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:14.380
which has been extended
into February.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.016
11 -- count them -- 11 shows
at the Beacon Theatre

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:17.883
here in New York City.

00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:19.285
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:21.988
our very good friend
Amy Poehler, everybody.

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:24.690
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:41.373 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:43.209
-Hi, Seth!
-Hello!

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:45.811
-Hello!
-The city just feels better

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:48.214
to have you back in it.
-Oh, it's so nice to be here

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.849
on the last show of the year
for you.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:50.916
-The last show of the year,
yeah.

00:00:50.916 --> 00:00:52.852
-Big show.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.221
-You're someone -- I've
known you for a long time.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.323
I feel like you
do take the turning

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:58.524
into a new year seriously.

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:00.226
You do see it as an opportunity.
-I do.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.094
I have a lot of thoughts
about it I want to talk about,

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:04.264
but I do want to say thank you
for having me on,

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.465
'cause I know
this is an important show

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:08.501
and Christmas show and, like --
-Yeah, we put

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.036
a lot of thought into it.
-A lot of thought.

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:12.405
And when you guys
called me yesterday...

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.807
-Yeah.
-...to do it --

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:17.777
-It was a yesterday situation.
-It felt great.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:18.778
It felt great.
-Yeah.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.446
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:21.914
-It was a real
bait and switch too,

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:24.150
because we were making
dinner plans with you.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.319
-We had dinner last night,
as normal friends.

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:27.620
-Yeah.
-With Mike Shoemaker,

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.155
producer on the show,
and, um...

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:32.891
we were --
And then I'm just curious.

00:01:32.891 --> 00:01:35.294
You don't have to name
any names, but what happened?

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:37.430
Like, who was supposed
to be here?

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.031
-Are you saying that maybe
there was somebody

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:40.299
who was scheduled to be here,

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:41.600
and they dropped out
last minute?

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.703
-I just have a feeling that you
guys don't usually

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.405
do a day-of bookings.
-Yeah.

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:47.607
-But...
-It's kind of

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.876
my improv background
is, like, to day-of it.

00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:52.512
-Yeah. Okay.
-I love to, like, day-of it.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:53.947
-Ugh!

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.581
Don't lean in when you say --
[ Chuckles ]

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:58.384
-Well, I don't know.
-But I'm thrilled to be here.

00:01:58.384 --> 00:01:59.619
I feel like
the neighbor next door...

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:00.853
-Yes.
-...and you're borrowing

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:03.489
my sugar.
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.224
-Yes.
-Thank you for that.

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:07.626
-But we are gearing up
on the holidays.

00:02:07.626 --> 00:02:08.861
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:10.496
Oh, your parents
are actually here.

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:12.999
Bill and Eileen Poehler
are in the audience tonight.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:16.135
-In the audience.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:17.570
-I've known them
for a very long time.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.205
-You have, you have.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.073
Hilary and Larry
and Bill and Eileen

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:22.608
have gone to many "SNL" shows.

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:24.410
-Very, very --
A lot of after parties.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.745
-Yeah. And, um, we have --
-I think both

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:29.048
of our dads think that
if Lorne Michaels had met them

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:30.616
when they were
a little bit younger,

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:31.750
they would have been
on the show, yeah.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:32.885
-They would
have been on the show.

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.754
But, um, we have a tradition.

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:37.423
My parents come to town
and we take the kids and we go

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:40.025
and we haggle
for the Christmas tree.

00:02:40.025 --> 00:02:41.928
-Oh, you buy
the Christmas tree together?

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.229
-Yeah.
-So who's the head haggler

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.132
of your family?
-My father is head haggler.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.967
-Yeah.
-Um, haggler extraordinaire,

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.536
and he goes and he goes
to the Christmas tree guy,

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:53.038
a wonderful guy
that we buy from every year,

00:02:53.038 --> 00:02:56.408
and he's like, "200 bucks?
I'm out of here!"

00:02:56.408 --> 00:02:58.210
And, you know,
the guy plays along

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.479
and he's like, "No, wait!

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:01.814
You're a great customer!
I got to get you this tree!"

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.516
And, um, we do it every year.

00:03:04.516 --> 00:03:08.053
And when the kids were little,
they were blown away.

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:09.255
-Yeah.
-They were like,

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.523
"We almost didn't get a tree."

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.758
Like...
-Right.

00:03:12.758 --> 00:03:14.093
-And now I think
they're catching on.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.195
-Now they're like,
"Oh, I'm watching a show.

00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:17.797
This is like a panto."

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.832
-That is our nativity scene.
That's what --

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:23.736
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Um, so you've got Christmas

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:25.204
and then you got New Year's.
-New Year's.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:26.738
You have your resolutions?
Resolutions?

00:03:26.738 --> 00:03:28.041
-Fred, are you ready to go?
-Fred, do you have any?

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.241
-Resolutions?
-Yes.

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:30.543
-Great.
-Okay. Great.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:31.610
-Yeah.
-We don't want to --

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:32.912
I don't want to know them.

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.313
-Fred, if you don't mind,
we don't want to hear it.

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:36.883
-I don't want to hear them.
We don't want to hear them.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:40.920
But I wrote some resolutions.
-Yeah?

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.321
-I wrote some resolutions.
-Great.

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:44.256
-If it's okay, I want to
share them with the audience

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:45.491
so you guys can support me.
-Yes.

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.859
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Alright.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.327
-Are you confident?

00:03:48.327 --> 00:03:50.596
Are you confident you're going
to keep your resolutions?

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:53.332
-Uh, I didn't put that
as a resolution, so no.

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:55.501
-Okay.
-But let's see.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.470
Let's see if it happens.
Um, okay.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:01.440
So in 2024, when people say,
"Hey, how's it going?"

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.709
instead of saying "good,"

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:05.178
I'm going to say,
"I'm doing the best that I can."

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:06.913
-Oh, that's good.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.047
-And I think that that --
-That's good.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:11.584
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah. Okay.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:15.287
Um, in 2024 I'm going
to continue to ignore cats.

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:17.856
-Okay.
-Because -- [ Laughs ]

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:20.893
-Yeah.
-I like them fine.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.263
-Yeah.
-You know, everyone

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:25.964
should treat cats wonderfully.
-Yeah.

00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:28.601
-And I get --
I'm a little bit allergic,

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:31.570
but if you ignore them,
they become obsessed with you.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:32.838
-Oh!
-So every time I go over

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:34.340
to someone's house,
the cat jumps in my lap,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.075
it, like, gets in my purse

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.010
and wants me to take it home
because I ignore it, so...

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.845
-So, you're not ignoring it
'cause you hate cats.

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:42.014
You're playing hard to get
to sort of covet...

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:43.716
Wow.
-Maybe.

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.851
[ Laughter ]
Okay.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:48.420
Um, Seth, this year, next year,
I'm going to create

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.256
some new slang,
and I want to try it out here.

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:51.957
-Oh, great!
We love new slang here.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:53.325
-[ Exhales sharply ]

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:55.795
Um, instead of instead
of calling someone a dummy...

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.629
-Yeah?
-...I think we should call him

00:04:57.629 --> 00:05:01.100
a ding dong.
-Okay.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.236
-Which isn't that new, but I
don't think it's used enough.

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:05.137
Like, what, a ding dong.
-Yeah, kids aren't using it.

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:06.873
-Kids aren't using it.
-And I think it would be --

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:09.075
Bringing it back into vogue
would be very nice.

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:12.244
-But I know that slang needs
to mean two things, right?

00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:13.479
-Right.
-Like, it can't just

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.414
mean one thing anymore,
so I also think ding dong

00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:18.984
would be a good thing
to replace "What's up?"

00:05:18.984 --> 00:05:20.819
-Oh, okay.
-So, like, if you don't

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:22.888
hear from your friend,
you just text him,

00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:25.157
you go, "Ding dong."
-I see.

00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:26.526
-"Ding dong."

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:28.294
And then you can go,
"Ding dong, ding dong.

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:29.561
-Oh, there you go.

00:05:29.561 --> 00:05:31.497
Both uses.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:33.166
-So...
-Yeah. Ding dong. I like that.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:34.567
You're sort of
like knocking at the door

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:35.601
of their psyche, being like,

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.302
"Hello?"
-[ Laughs ] Exactly!

00:05:37.302 --> 00:05:38.604
-"Ding dong. Knock knock."

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.372
-"Knock knock." Yeah.
It's like knock --

00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:43.408
Yeah, it's a little bit
like "knock knock."

00:05:43.408 --> 00:05:44.677
But it's cool. It's cool.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.745
I'm just going to get
Travis Scott to say it

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:47.713
and we'll be good.
-Okay.

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:48.881
-Um, okay.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:51.016
I'm going to end
all my work e-mails with...

00:05:51.016 --> 00:05:51.984
"Oops!"

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.987
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:57.456
-And you're not worried
that people might reread

00:05:57.456 --> 00:05:59.791
the e-mail
to try to find a mistake?

00:05:59.791 --> 00:06:02.127
-No, I just think
it's going to cover me HR-wise,

00:06:02.127 --> 00:06:04.563
and I think that...
I think that'll be good.

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:05.664
-That's great.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.900
-Oh, um, I'm going
to start a cult.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:08.868
-Oh, great.
-Yeah.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.470
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.671
-You'll be so good at it.
Can I just say --

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:13.339
-Thank you!

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:14.640
Well, let me ask you this.
-Okay.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:15.975
-I'm going to go off the list.
-Okay.

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:17.643
-I'm gonna get off the page.
-Okay, great.

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.478
-Um, do you think
you would be more apt

00:06:19.478 --> 00:06:21.681
to join a cult or start a cult?

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:23.416
-I'm a joiner.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:25.284
This is --
-100%.

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:26.719
Like, I actually think...
-Fred?

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:28.621
-I think I'm a joiner, too.
-I think you are, too.

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:31.190
-I think the main thing for me
in a cult is, like,

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.659
I want to stop
making any decisions.

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.661
-Yeah. Well, good news.

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:37.396
In my cult,
you're not going to need

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.398
to make any decisions.
-Okay.

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.200
I would always pick you
as a starter,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.168
not a joiner, FYI.
-100%.

00:06:43.168 --> 00:06:45.337
And what I love so much
about cults is how --

00:06:45.337 --> 00:06:46.472
it's like
a Tim Robinson sketch,

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.474
like how the rules come in,

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:49.975
and at first
they sound really good

00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:52.377
and they always end up
with like, "And then -- Huh.

00:06:52.377 --> 00:06:55.982
I guess rule seven is, like,
everybody have sex with me."

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.582
Like --
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:57.582 --> 00:07:00.018
It always gets to that.
-Yeah.

00:07:00.018 --> 00:07:02.821
-But in my cult it's going
to be more like mothering.

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:04.357
Like, there was this
really good documentary.

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:05.458
I don't know if you saw it.

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:07.993
It was about --
I won't mention the cult

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:09.929
because I don't want to
bring up the "competish."

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:11.831
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, of course.

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:14.600
-But it was all about like
mother, mother, mother god.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:15.935
-Oh.
[ Woman screams ]

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:17.403
-Mother god.
-Oh, so either some

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:18.738
former members
or they saw the doc.

00:07:18.738 --> 00:07:21.307
[ Laughter ]
It must be so exciting.

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:22.875
Do you think
when you get out of a cult --

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:24.342
Obviously it's a bad time.
-Yeah.

00:07:24.342 --> 00:07:26.144
-But when you get out,
do you still have that thing,

00:07:26.144 --> 00:07:27.947
like, it's your hometown
where somebody mentions it

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:29.648
and you can't help it going...
[ Both cheer ]

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:31.149
-That's my coll--
I mean, that's bad.

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:32.418
-That was bad, but...

00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:33.819
I met good friends.

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:35.888
It was bad,
but I made a lot of friends.

00:07:35.888 --> 00:07:37.757
-You're right.
I still tailgate at the cult.

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:39.892
-Yeah.
-But, um, anyway,

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:42.494
I want to be like a mother --
like mother.

00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:43.996
I want to be called
some kind of mother.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:46.131
-Okay.
-And I what I liked about that

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:48.601
particular cult is that when --

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.836
when, um,
the cult leader was tired,

00:07:50.836 --> 00:07:52.371
everyone was like,
"Mother is resting."

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:54.840
-Yeah.
-Just a few more, Seth.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.442
-That's great.
-I'm going to make

00:07:56.442 --> 00:07:59.612
one robot friend next year.
-Oh.

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:01.647
-And I suggest everyone does.
-Okay.

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:03.682
Now does, like -- Can I ask --
Well, go ahead.

00:08:03.682 --> 00:08:05.050
-I feel like -- Can I ask?
-Yeah.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:06.151
-Like, does Siri count,

00:08:06.151 --> 00:08:07.686
or do you need,
like, an actual robot?

00:08:07.686 --> 00:08:09.055
-I think it's going
to have to count

00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:10.789
'cause I don't know
how many actual robots

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:12.891
I'm going to get
to meet next year.

00:08:12.891 --> 00:08:15.361
So Alexa, Siri,
I feel like that will count.

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:17.963
But I do think we're going to
need to have at least one friend

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:20.065
when the war starts.
-Yeah.

00:08:20.065 --> 00:08:22.734
To, like, vouch for you?
-To whatever.

00:08:22.734 --> 00:08:24.436
Vouch, translate. Hide us.

00:08:24.436 --> 00:08:26.305
Whatever it is,
like, we're going to --

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:29.308
So I think we're going to need
to get rid of human friends

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:31.177
and make more robot friends.
-Okay.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:33.479
-And my GPS has
an Australian accent

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:34.947
for some reason.
-Uh-huh.

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:37.115
-I don't know why, so I'm going
to try to make friends with her.

00:08:37.115 --> 00:08:39.184
-Okay, great.
-Um, okay.

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:42.221
-Can you do real quick
your GPS telling you to turn?

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:46.291
Wait, do you have to do that
face before you do...?

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:48.493
-Australia face!
-I mean, by the way, can I say,

00:08:48.493 --> 00:08:50.228
the minute you did it,
I'm like, "Australian."

00:08:50.228 --> 00:08:52.498
If I -- If I walked in --

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:54.266
-[ Speaks indistinctly
in Australian accent ]

00:08:54.266 --> 00:08:55.834
-What?
-[ Indistinct ]

00:08:55.834 --> 00:08:59.872
-Razor blades? Why does
your GPS say razor blades?

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:04.810
-When you say "raise up lights,"

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.346
it sounds like "razor blades"
in Australian.

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:09.048
[ Australian accent ]
Rise up lights.

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:10.716
-Oh, that's good. That's like --

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:13.251
Did you ever hear
that "air hair lair"

00:09:13.251 --> 00:09:15.120
is how to do a good
fancy British person

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:16.188
saying "oh, hello there."

00:09:16.188 --> 00:09:17.757
Air hair lair!

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:19.658
[ Laughter ]

00:09:19.658 --> 00:09:20.793
-Alright.
You do your British.

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:22.694
I'll do my Australian.
-Okay.

00:09:22.694 --> 00:09:26.032
-Razor blades!
-Air hair lair!

00:09:26.032 --> 00:09:28.167
[ Laughter ]

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:29.134
-That's good.

00:09:29.134 --> 00:09:32.805
-Can I --
-That was like a PBS...

00:09:32.805 --> 00:09:35.674
-That was our master class
right there.

00:09:35.674 --> 00:09:38.810
-That was like a PBS show.
That was so good. Um, okay.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:40.145
And then, lastly, I have

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:42.914
a couple quick resolutions
for you if you're ready.

00:09:42.914 --> 00:09:44.850
-You've written my resolutions?

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.485
-Yeah, "a few for you, Seth."

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:48.554
That's what it says.
You ready? Okay.

00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:51.389
Number one, I think next year

00:09:51.389 --> 00:09:52.858
you should start tipping.

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:57.363
[ Laughter ]

00:09:57.363 --> 00:09:59.231
-I just -- Can I say my thing?

00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:01.433
I mean, you've been at dinner
with me. You've heard me say it.

00:10:01.433 --> 00:10:02.801
The money is
for all of it, though.

00:10:02.801 --> 00:10:05.137
-No, Seth, we've gone over this.
-Why?

00:10:05.137 --> 00:10:07.773
Just put whatever -- Price it
into the hamburger.

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:10.075
-Think about it.
Think about tipping next year.

00:10:10.075 --> 00:10:12.644
-Okay.
-Um. Okay.

00:10:12.644 --> 00:10:15.013
You know -- I know you're going
to disagree with me on that.

00:10:15.013 --> 00:10:17.583
You know how they say dance
like nobody's watching?

00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:20.052
I think maybe you should
start dancing like people

00:10:20.052 --> 00:10:22.788
are watching.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:24.122
-Just, like, keep it in mind.

00:10:24.122 --> 00:10:25.524
-Just because people
are watching.

00:10:25.524 --> 00:10:26.959
You're very famous.
You have a show.

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:28.494
Like, people are watching.
-Were you --

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:31.029
You know, at some
"SNL" after parties, I mean,

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:32.198
if there was
a catch phrase you had

00:10:32.198 --> 00:10:33.499
at the after party,
it was you being like,

00:10:33.499 --> 00:10:35.567
[whispering]
"People are watching you."

00:10:35.567 --> 00:10:38.070
-I would grab you and go,
"People are watching." Yeah.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.371
And then the last one is

00:10:39.371 --> 00:10:41.340
I think you should
make a robot friend.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:42.641
-Okay.
-And then I think we should

00:10:42.641 --> 00:10:44.276
introduce them to each other
and then we could have

00:10:44.276 --> 00:10:45.777
a robot wedding!

00:10:45.777 --> 00:10:46.946
A robot wedding!
-Oh, my God,

00:10:46.946 --> 00:10:48.347
a robot wedding would be so fun.

00:10:48.347 --> 00:10:51.217
-And robot babies!
-Oh.

00:10:51.217 --> 00:10:52.785
-And we could -- We could
officiate a robot wedding.

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:54.619
-This was, um --
Can I say, you are,

00:10:54.619 --> 00:10:57.055
I mean, one of the great
last-minute guests of all time.

00:10:57.055 --> 00:10:58.757
That was fantastic.
-Thank you guys so much.

00:10:58.757 --> 00:11:00.225
-So good.
-I wrote this in the car.

00:11:00.225 --> 00:11:03.295
I wrote this in the car.
-So good that

00:11:03.295 --> 00:11:05.097
you did not
even plug yourself.

00:11:05.097 --> 00:11:06.298
But I just want to say
very quickly,

00:11:06.298 --> 00:11:08.367
this is my favorite podcast
of the year.

00:11:08.367 --> 00:11:10.936
"Say More with Dr? Sheila."
Check it out.

00:11:10.936 --> 00:11:13.205
[ Cheers and applause ]
If you live in New York City,

00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:15.474
go see these two
at the Beacon Theatre.

00:11:15.474 --> 00:11:17.709
They're gonna do 11 shows in
February, and they're doing --

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:18.711
you're doing shows
all over the country.

00:11:18.711 --> 00:11:19.812
-It's been so fun.

00:11:19.812 --> 00:11:21.113
We're just partying on the road.

00:11:21.113 --> 00:11:22.714
We're going nonstop.
-Yeah.

00:11:22.714 --> 00:11:25.384
-We do a show, we get right
home, we take a bath,

00:11:25.384 --> 00:11:28.420
we go to Bloomingdale's
the next day, every day.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:33.158
Rinse, repeat. Show, bedtime.
Bloomingdale's, show...!

00:11:33.158 --> 00:11:35.026
-Wow! The road!
-Yeah. The road.

00:11:35.026 --> 00:11:36.628
-I love you very much.

00:11:36.628 --> 00:11:37.830
-I love you.
-Thank you for being here.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.365
-Amy Poehler, everybody!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:40.365 --> 00:11:42.201
"Say More with Dr? Sheila"
is available now.

00:11:42.201 --> 00:11:44.002
And for more info
on The Restless Leg Tour,

00:11:44.002 --> 00:11:45.737
go to tinaamytour.com.

00:11:45.737 --> 00:11:47.273
We'll be right back
with Jack Antonoff.

